        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.736,
            0.047,
            0.846,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Number Play"
        },
        {
          "type": "doc_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.406,
            0.1,
            0.591,
            0.124
          ],
          "level": 1,
          "content": "SUMMARY"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.138,
            0.613,
            0.157
          ],
          "content": "In this chapter, we have explored the following:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.158,
            0.823,
            0.214
          ],
          "content": "In the first activity, we saw how to represent information about how a sequence of numbers (e.g., height measures) is arranged without knowing the actual numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.215,
            0.823,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "We learnt the notion of parity — numbers that can be arranged in pairs (even numbers) and numbers that cannot be arranged in pairs (odd numbers)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.273,
            0.758,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": "• We learnt how to determine the parity of sums and products."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.292,
            0.823,
            0.348
          ],
          "content": "While exploring sums in grids, we could determine whether filling a grid is impossible by looking at the row and column sums. We extended this to construct magic squares."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.35,
            0.823,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "We saw how Virahāṅka numbers were first discovered in history through the Arts. The Virahāṅka sequence is 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34, 55, ..."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.407,
            0.822,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": "We became math-detectives through cryptarithms, where digits are replaced by letters."
        },
        {
